Koru tracks obligations on two ledgers. The comptime ledger commits `[witness] site custody-edge` lines while the flow graph is checked; the runtime record appends events to a world-mounted `holds.log` the bridge can read back. Both replay the same lifecycle — `mint`, `held`, `borrow`, `discharge`, `issued` — and both had the same hole: an obligation that gets *exercised* without changing hands left no trace. "Borrowed live" read exactly like "never touched."

The vocabulary now has five ops, and the fifth is exercise:

```koru
tor open { path: string } -> string<opened!>
tor poke { conn: string<opened> } -> string
tor shut { conn: string<!opened> } -> string
```

`open` returns `<opened!>` — a minted debt. `shut` takes `<!opened>` — it consumes. `poke` takes bare `<opened>` — a borrow: it needs the handle alive but leaves custody with the caller. The signature was always the spec; the ledgers just never read it.

## Borrow: the comptime edge

Passing a live obligation into a bare `<state>` parameter commits a `borrow` edge at the call site — named on the *arg* binding, because what the edge describes is the caller's obligation being lent, not the callee's parameter:

```koru
tor peek { h: *Handle<owned> }
```

The ledger rule is the one the signature already implied: `borrow` is legal only against an outstanding `minted` or `held` obligation, and it leaves the state untouched — an exercise record, not a transfer. The witness corpus pins the shape directly:

```text
[witness] site custody-edge h0 h0<-input:*Handle<input:owned!> mint
[witness] site custody-edge h0 h0<-input:*Handle<input:owned!> borrow
[witness] site custody-edge h0 h0<-input:*Handle<input:owned!> discharge
```

One placement rule keeps the channel honest: only obligation-carrying args edge. A flow-impl's own borrow parameter owes nothing in its scope, so a re-passed borrow stays silent — the ledger can't be inflated by forwarding a handle through helper after helper.

