  test={{ name: "330_146 custody edges, all five ops", directory: "330_146_custody_edge_witnesses_mint_held_issued", categorySlug: "300_ADVANCED_FEATURES/330_PHANTOM_TYPES", description: "peek exercises h0 between mint and discharge; fwd relays a held obligation through borrow into issued: mint=5 held=2 borrow=2 discharge=5 issued=2." }}
/>

## Issued: the relay that found a second ledger

Exercising `held` → `issued` exposed a gap on the compiler side, not the Lean side. A chain-terminal produce (`x: _ -> h`) exits through a different checker path than an arm-leaf `-> expr`, and it never ran the escape audit — a minted `-> h` settled invisibly. Held obligations hid deeper still: they live outside the tracked obligation set entirely (the contract settles them invisibly), so no audit keyed on that set could see one escape. Both are covered now — one shared audit reads the tracked set for minted escapes and scans held bindings the produce names: carried through a declared `!` return → `issued`, named but un-carried → the same evaporation refusal the minted path enforces.

## Use: the runtime twin

The world-mounted record needed the same fix from the other side. A `use` event fires where the borrow is *admitted* — the dispatch point that already proved the handle live — so a `use` line on a dead handle can only mean the gate regressed. A refused borrow writes nothing: the wire already carries the refusal, the record carries granted exercises.

```text
world< {"branch":"","value":"a: closed — 1 minted, 1 released\nb: open — current session, holding nothing\nclean: 6 events, ledger settled — 'b' live"}
```

Session `a` mints `conn_1`, pokes it, shuts it. Session `b` reads the settled span and tries its own `poke` on the released handle — refused at dispatch, and the record stays clean: six events, no phantom seventh.

