## Two readers, one vocabulary

Neither ledger is self-checked. `WitnessCheck.lean` re-decides the comptime corpus — including `borrow` only from an outstanding `minted`/`held` state, and a bare-proto borrow line as malformed like every other op. `HoldsCheck.lean` replays the runtime record — `use` on a released or unminted handle names the violation (`use-of-released`, `use-of-unminted`, `use-outside-session`), and its settlement theorem now covers exercise: every release *and* every use must be backed by a live mint. Divergence between the runtime render and the Lean re-decision on the same corpus is itself a finding.

Two limits worth naming. The ledger records granted exercises — a borrow refused upstream of admission is only on the wire, not the record. And the `held` → `issued` relay exists only where a produce *names* the held binding: untouched held obligations stay contract-open, since the host impl's contract settles them invisibly. Naming one through a return with no declared `!` channel is the evaporation refusal — pinned in the twin test below.

<RegressionTestLink
  test={{ name: "330_151 held produced without carrier refuses", directory: "330_151_held_obligation_produced_without_carrier_refuses", categorySlug: "300_ADVANCED_FEATURES/330_PHANTOM_TYPES", description: "fwd produces its held h through a bare *Handle return — the obligation would evaporate, so KORU030 fires at the produce exit." }}
